vaporOS NuttX patch: cdcacm-tx-ring-race

Problem:   The USB CDC-ACM console hangs under bursts of output (a tab-completion
           list, vi's screen paint): output stops mid-stream and input is never
           answered again; only a replug recovers.
Evidence:  Reproduced every time by `vi <file>` (a screen paint of ~800 bytes in
           many small writes). The same paint completes, and the editor works,
           with this change. cdcacm_sndpacket() ends in
           uart_xmitchars_dma(), which fills a request from the TX ring and then
           advances the ring tail. It is called both from the writing thread
           (cdcuart_txint, via uart_write) and from the bulk-IN completion
           interrupt (cdcacm_wrcomplete). MECHANISM (read from the code, not confirmed
           by tracing): an interrupt arriving inside the
           thread's call re-ran the setup from the same, not-yet-advanced
           tail: two requests consumed the same bytes and the tail was
           advanced twice, corrupting the ring and wedging TX.
Fix:       run the `txfree` test and uart_xmitchars_dma() inside a critical
           section.
Upstream:  YES, likely. It is a plain reentrancy bug in the generic CDC-ACM
           driver (not RP2040 specific; affects any board with CDCACM and TX DMA
           mode). Needs a minimal reproducer that does not need vaporOS (a
           loop of small writes to /dev/ttyACM0 on any board) and a check
           of whether upstream NuttX master still has the window; also consider
           a spinlock rather than a global critical section.

Apply: git -C ../nuttx apply ../vaporOS/patches/nuttx/cdcacm-tx-ring-race.patch   (setup.sh does this)

diff --git a/drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c b/drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c
index 5b003218fb..44ec338519 100644
--- a/drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c
+++ b/drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c
@@ -485,10 +485,21 @@ static int cdcacm_sndpacket(FAR struct cdcacm_dev_s *priv)
 
   spin_unlock_irqrestore_nopreempt(&priv->lock, flags);
 #else
+  /* vaporOS: this runs both from a writing thread (txint) and from the
+   * bulk-IN completion interrupt.  Setting up the DMA transfer from the TX
+   * ring is not reentrant: an interrupt in the middle made two requests
+   * consume (and advance past) the same bytes, corrupting the ring and
+   * stalling the console under bursts of output.
+   */
+
+  irqstate_t txflags = enter_critical_section();
+
   if (!sq_empty(&priv->txfree))
     {
       uart_xmitchars_dma(&priv->serdev);
     }
+
+  leave_critical_section(txflags);
 #endif
 
